  "plot_summary": "The story picks up immediately after the conclusion of <i>Fantastic Four</i> #26, and the Avengers return home to inspect the damage.&nbsp; A construction crew is summoned, while the various Avengers head back to their respective homes to take care of personal matters, Rick Jones heading off with Captain America, who promises to teach him some acrobatic moves.\n\nBut only \"a few days later,\" at Stark's Flushing, Long Island plant, a mysterious sound is detected in the earth.&nbsp; It is also heard inside an ant hill that Giant-Man and the Wasp are investigating.&nbsp; And Don Blake is at that moment reading a newspaper article about strange sounds causing a train accident.&nbsp; It's happening everywhere, so Thor decides to summon the Avengers.&nbsp; He personally goes to collect Captain America, and soon, the Avengers are concluding that the strange sounds are originating out in the southwest.\n\nSure enough, out near where the Hulk is often seen, a strange mound has General Ross mystified, even as Dr. Bruce Banner finally returns to the installation after months.&nbsp; Ross asks him to look into the mystery.\n\nThe reader is finally shown the cause - the Lava Men are pushing \"living rock\" up toward the surface world.&nbsp; Somehow this will menace the \"surface men\" and save the Lava Men.&nbsp; Only one of them objects, he being the \"Lava Man\" that Thor faced in <i>Journey Into Mystery</i> 97 ([LINK:1963:JIM_97_thor0|JIM #97|2026-12-16]), who is given the name \"Molto\" here.&nbsp; The witch doctor seems intent on \"war between us and the surface people!\"&nbsp; The leader of the Lava Men ignores Molto's advice to stop.\n\nOn the surface, just before General Ross can attack the mound (which is apparently this \"living rock\"), the Avengers arrive on the scene.&nbsp; Iron Man tests the rock with his repulsers [sic - yes, in these earlier issues, it's still spelled with an 'e'], which is felt below by the Lava Men, and they think they're being attacked!&nbsp; Iron Man is overwhelmed by the heat the Lava Men put out, and Thor arrives to help, apparently immune to the intense heat.&nbsp; He addresses the ruler.\n\nThor learns that the \"living rock\" is growing bigger each day and emits a noise that damages anything it strikes - they're attempting to push it up to the surface world!&nbsp; The ruler reveals that they found it a long time ago when it was small, and that any attempt to strike it causes catastrophic explosions.&nbsp; It's to a size now that any attack on it could destroy the entire Earth, so the Lava Men are pushing it up to the surface in the hope that, if it explodes, most of the destruction will blow out into space, keeping them safe below the surface!\n\nThor knows he dare not strike the stone, and the witch doctor then orders the Lava Men to head to the surface and attack!&nbsp; While they get into it, Giant-Man and the Wasp have been studying the stone and identify a spot where it might crack without exploding and need Thor to strike that spot with his hammer.\n\nBut at that moment, Bruce Banner watches on and reverts to the Hulk.&nbsp; He sees his enemies, the Avengers, and attacks!&nbsp; At the same time, the witch doctor attacks Thor, keeping him from getting back to the rock.&nbsp; Instead, the Avengers manipulate the Hulk into striking the one weak spot in the rock, which <i>implodes</i> as a result, instead of <i>exploding</i> - the Earth is saved!\n\nThor commands the Lava Men to return to their homes...<i>or else</i>!&nbsp; He seals up the entrance to their underground realm.&nbsp; The Avengers pick themselves up and find that the Hulk has vanished yet again, and Betty finds Banner lying nearby.&nbsp; And as the Avengers are discussing their apparent victory, an urgent summons suddenly comes in from the Teen Brigade.&nbsp; It's a \"condition red\" emergency!&nbsp; The Avengers race off!&nbsp; (I guess the Hulk's whereabouts are again just not important enough to give a second thought to - <i>wow</i>).",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME - write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME - write something",
  "review_text": "A pretty lackluster story involving a dull opponent and, save for the one moment where they maneuver the Hulk into position, they don't even work together as a team.&nbsp; The Hulk's presence seems to be quite random, as if Stan Lee had not fully decided to eliminate him from the mag.&nbsp; (In fact, his head is still in the upper-left corner of the cover, along with the actual members of the Avengers, not to mention being drawn as part of the group going up against the Lava Men - which of course did not <i>happen</i> in the story - heh, creative license strikes again!)&nbsp; And the Hulk conveniently turns back into Bruce Banner after his one blow, which caused the day to be saved.&nbsp; A sudden implosion does not seem like it would have that effect at all!&nbsp; It would only make him more angry.&nbsp; Once again, we have the character pulled in only as demanded by the plot, which is, in the end, not a good enough reason to include him.\n\nAs for the Lava Men, ugh, these races of people that live below the surface stretch believability, particularly since they don't run into each other (at least yet).&nbsp; We have the Lava Men here (the second appearance of their race - recall [LINK:1963:JIM_97_thor0|JIM #97|2026-12-16]), the Mole Man's kingdom of Subterranea (see [LINK:1963:FF_22|FF #22|2027-02-17]) and the descendants of the people of Atlantis from [LINK:1963:TOS_43_iron0|TOS #43], just to name some that I can remember off-hand.&nbsp; And naturally, all of them seem to want to invade the surface world!\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nThis is a yawn-inducing story.&nbsp; Jack Kirby's art was fine as with most of his offerings, but there wasn't much going on worth reading about...\n\nThe flashback scene by the Lava Men's ruler begins with \"It all started many moons ago...\"&nbsp; Interesting that they would keep time by \"moons\" when they live below the surface and don't even see the moon by which they seem to mark off time...",
  "highlight": "FIXME - write something"
}